{"id":586,"slug":"indiscretion-of-an-american-wife","title":"Indiscretion of an American Wife","year":1953,"country":"United States, Italy","duration":72,"spine":202,"url":"https://www.criterion.com/films/586-indiscretion-of-an-american-wife","cover":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/criterion-production/films/88efe0fd7022bda95b58775ad4f6e8a9/3dXWG1WeyQutBTTjGG4FJuSBJaOKxN_large.jpg","description":"An American housewife (Jennifer Jones) vacationing in Italy reluctantly decides to put an end to her brief affair with an Italian academic (Montgomery Clift). She flees to Rome’s Stazione Termini, where she bids him farewell, but he begs her to stay. The film’s plot is simple; its production was not. The troubled collaboration between director Vittorio De Sica and producer David O. Selznick resulted in two cuts of the same film. De Sica’s version, Terminal Station, was screened at a length of one-and-a-half hours, but after disappointing previews, Selznick severely re-edited it and changed the title to Indiscretion of an American Wife without De Sica’s permission. The Criterion Collection is proud to present both versions of this controversial release.","boxset":0,"scraped":1,"directors":[{"name":"Vittorio De Sica","slug":"vittorio-de-sica"}],"cast":[{"actor":"Montgomery Clift","role":"Giovanni Doria","slug":"montgomery-clift"},{"actor":"Jennifer Jones","role":"Mary Forbes","slug":"jennifer-jones"},{"actor":"Richard Beymer","role":"Paul","slug":"richard-beymer"}],"credits":[{"role":"Director","person":"Vittorio De Sica","slug":"vittorio-de-sica"},{"role":"Associate producers","person":"Marcello Girosi","slug":null},{"role":"Associate producers","person":"Wolfgang Reinhardt","slug":null},{"role":"Music","person":"Alessandro Cicognini","slug":null},{"role":"Conducted by","person":"Franco Ferrara","slug":null},{"role":"Photographed by","person":"Aldo Graziati","slug":null},{"role":"Editing","person":"Eraldo Da Roma","slug":null},{"role":"Miss Jones' costumes designed by","person":"C